Let $A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.$  Then
\begin{align*}
A^2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \\
A^3 &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \\
A^4 &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 5 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}In general, $A^n = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ n & 1 \end{pmatrix}.$  Therefore,
\begin{align*}
A^{2018} &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2018 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}

\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2018 & 1 \end{pmatrix}}

The volume of a sphere is given by the formula $V = \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3$.  Since the volume of the larger ball is equal to the sum of the volumes of the eight smaller balls, we have
\[\frac{4}{3}\pi R^3 = 8 \cdot \frac{4}{3}\pi (1)^3,\]where $R$ is the radius of the larger ball.  Dividing both sides by $\frac{4}{3}\pi$, we get $R^3 = 8$, so $R = \sqrt[3]{8} = 2$.

